# What this repository contains, and under which terms

The benchmark draws on 46 upstream projects, which are under at least
Apache-2.0, LGPL, GPL-3.0, BSD-3-Clause, MIT and zlib terms.

## Ours, covered by LICENSE

- the QuartetFuzz system (`agent/`, `harness/`, `infra/`, `scripts/`,
  `reproduce.sh`)
- the benchmark's case records (`benchmark/benchmark_100.jsonl`,
  `benchmark/<task>/case.json`), Logic Groups, P2 reports, selection protocol,
  measurements (reference coverage) and tools (`benchmark/tools/`)
- the harnesses QuartetFuzz generated (`results/harnesses/QuartetFuzz/`)

## Not ours

- **OSS-Fuzz project files** in `benchmark/<task>/image/` and
  `qf-project/` (Dockerfile, build.sh, project.yaml, and the files OSS-Fuzz
  ships with each project) come from google/oss-fuzz at commit 955c686 under
  Apache-2.0, with our pinning and stubbing added.  Where OSS-Fuzz keeps the
  reference harness in the project directory, that public file is part of
  the build context; the image build replaces it with a stub, so no system
  sees it.
- **Baseline output.** `results/harnesses/OSS-Fuzz-Gen/` and `PromeFuzz/` were
  generated by those tools (Apache-2.0 and their respective terms) on our runs.
- **Corpora** are not in this repository.  Each task's
  `gold/global_corpus/manifest.json` records the URL OSS-Fuzz published it at,
  the seed count and the SHA-256 of the archive we measured; OSS-Fuzz replaces
  these archives daily, so the digest identifies what we used but cannot be
  re-checked against the live URL.
- **The libraries** are fetched from upstream at the pinned revisions when a
  task image is built, under their own licences.
- **Upstream tools.** OSS-Fuzz-Gen and PromeFuzz are not included; the
  versions we ran are in separate repositories (see README).
